# Photograph the level table printed by five prestige classes.
#
# The tables are player-facing prose that sits in the same file as the class's
# own Flags and Def: field, so the three can disagree -- and in these five they
# did. See bd inc-tek.8.3, findings PA-03-F16 (three classes printed their save
# columns transposed) and PA-03-F17 (two printed a defence track their class
# does not grant). tools/check_prestige_tables.sh reads these dumps.
#
# The prestige menu is the right screen to read them from because it shows the
# class's Desc: exactly as a player choosing a class sees it. Nothing here
# takes a prestige class; the menu only highlights.
#
# HOW IT GETS THERE. Free Advancement is a wizard-mode switch, so the script
# enters wizard mode (lowercase w -- 'W' is a different keystroke, see
# TokenToKey in src/Wposix.cpp), turns the switch on, and then uses the
# character sheet's [G] to reach the three core levels that
# src/Managers.cpp:2252 demands before it will open the prestige list.
#
# SEED-INDEPENDENT for what it asserts. Each class is named rather than
# counted, so the number of prestige classes in lib/ does not matter, and the
# descriptions are static text. The chargen it includes is seed-locked, which
# is why a seed is still passed.

@include chargen-rogue.keys

ESC             # dismiss the arrival-room description
w               # wizard mode
y               # "Enter wizard mode?" -- yes
w               # [w] Wizard Mode Switches
DOWN DOWN       # Cheat Death -> Auto-Identify Items -> Free Advancement
SPACE           # NO -> YES
ESC             # leave the options manager

d               # character sheet
G G G           # three core levels; the prestige list refuses fewer

# WHY @cursorto AND NOT @choose. Pressing a menu letter TAKES the class:
# LMenu returns the moment its letter arrives, src/TextTerm.cpp:1217-1231.
# This script only reads the descriptions, and a character may hold one
# prestige class at most, so it must move the highlight without pressing a
# letter. @cursorto sends DOWN until the named entry is the highlighted one.
#
# This used to be counted -- DOWN*9 to reach the Loremaster -- and one class
# added to lib/prestige.irh moved every count after it. The script would then
# photograph a different class, under the old name, and pass.
#
# Each class then scrolls to its own table with @until, which is proof against
# the descriptions changing length. The extra PGDNs bring the rows themselves
# into view; the @until stops as soon as the header appears on the last line.
@choose "Prestige Classes"
@expect "Choose a class:"

@cursorto "Alienist" DOWN
@until "Special Abilities" PGDN
PGDN*8
@dump:alienist

@cursorto "Blackguard" DOWN
@until "Special Abilities" PGDN
PGDN*8
@dump:blackguard

@cursorto "Loremaster" DOWN
@until "Special Abilities" PGDN
PGDN*8
@dump:loremaster

@cursorto "Sentinel" DOWN
@until "Special Abilities" PGDN
PGDN*4                          # this table is four rows and then "..."
@dump:sentinel

@cursorto "Twilight Huntsman" DOWN
@until "Special Abilities" PGDN
PGDN*8
@dump:twilight

# Not a table: this one proves the naming survives a truncated menu. LMenu
# cuts every entry to the column width (src/TextTerm.cpp:1022), so this class
# is drawn "[e] Celestial Initiat" and the full name is nowhere on the screen.
# tools/check_key_directives.sh reads this dump.
@cursorto "Celestial Initiate" DOWN
@dump:truncated

ESC
@quit
